                            <comment id="489925" author="green" created="Thu, 5 Mar 2026 03:47:49 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=ksolanki&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;ksolanki&quot;&gt;ksolanki&lt;/a&gt; We need devops side support so we have something like rock9.7 or 10.x or some such with zfs-2.4 so we can make sure it really builds, but also could be tested against this version.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="492835" author="mkvardakov" created="Fri, 10 Apr 2026 14:08:14 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=green&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;green&quot;&gt;green&lt;/a&gt; I&apos;ve added 9.7+10.1 builders to CO-ES cluster. ES does not build though because MOFED drivers are not available for recent distros. Please help me to understand what changes we need to make&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="492874" author="green" created="Fri, 10 Apr 2026 17:13:02 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately I am not well versed in the mofed side of things, I suggest asking somebody om nthe lnet side? &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=cbordage&quot; class=&quot;user-hover&quot; rel=&quot;cbordage&quot;&gt;cbordage&lt;/a&gt;&#160; any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="492889" author="cbordage" created="Fri, 10 Apr 2026 21:47:35 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, I am not a mofed expert. This is what I found in NVidia documentation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;preformatted panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;preformattedContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;If your kernel version does not match with any of the offered 
pre-built RPMs, you can add your kernel version by using the 
&#8220;mlnx_add_kernel_support.sh&#8221; script located inside the MLNX_OFED 
package. &lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is a hack, and it may work or not on 9.7. But it would be very surprising if it works with 10.x.&lt;br/&gt;
I don&apos;t see any solution other than using in-kernel version.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
